# Tools &amp; architecture

This page explains *why* the project is set up the way it is and how the tools fit
together. It is understanding-oriented — for step-by-step instructions, see the
[how-to guide](../how-to/project-tasks.md), and for a quick lookup table see the
[tools reference](../reference/tools.md).

## The big picture

The tooling sits on four layers, each with a distinct job:

- **uv** is the engine. It manages the Python interpreter, the virtual
  environment, and dependencies (read from `pyproject.toml` and pinned in
  `uv.lock`). It does the actual installing and running.
- **pre-commit** is the quality gate. On every commit it runs the checks —
  formatting, linting, typing, security, tests — so problems are caught before
  they land in history.
- **just** is the command layer. It does no real work itself; it gives short,
  uniform names (`just test`, `just qa`) to the real commands underneath
  (`uv run pytest`, `pre-commit run …`). One vocabulary for humans, CI, and
  coding agents.
- **Copier** is the scaffolding layer, one level *above* the project. It generated
  this project from a template and can pull future template improvements back in
  via `copier update`.

A useful way to hold it: uv and pre-commit *do* the work, just standardizes *how*
you trigger it, and Copier governs *how the project is created and kept current*.

Copier and just are, strictly speaking, optional and replaceable — you could clone
the repo, run `uv sync`, and call the tools directly. What they buy you is
consistency across many projects (Copier) and a stable, discoverable command
surface (just). Copier is used rarely (at creation, then on template updates);
just is used every day.

## The tools in detail

### uv — environment &amp; dependencies

Manages the Python {{ python_version }} interpreter, the `.venv`, and all
dependencies. Runtime dependencies live under `[project.dependencies]`; tooling
lives in `[dependency-groups]` (`dev` and `docs`). `uv sync` makes the environment
match the lockfile; `uv run …` executes a command inside it.

### just — task runner

A thin wrapper that maps memorable recipes to real commands (see the `justfile`).
It is the single entry point that `AGENTS.md`, CI, and you all share, so "how do I
run the tests" has one answer in every project.

### Copier — project generator

Generated this project from a template by filling placeholders (name, package,
Python version, …) recorded in `.copier-answers.yml`. Running `copier update`
re-applies an updated template and merges improvements into the project.

### pre-commit — orchestrates the checks

Defined in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`. Remote hooks (Ruff, Bandit, Gitleaks,
hygiene checks) run in isolated environments; local hooks (mypy, pylint,
{% if include_sql %}sqlfluff, {% endif %}pytest) run in *your* environment so imports resolve correctly.
`default_language_version` is pinned to Python {{ python_version }}.

### Ruff — linter &amp; formatter

The fast, primary linter and code formatter (configured in `[tool.ruff]`). It
covers import sorting, modernizations, bug patterns, and Google-style docstrings,
and replaces the need for separate tools like isort/flake8/black.

### mypy — static type checking

Runs in `strict` mode (`[tool.mypy]`) and verifies type hints across `src` and
`tests`. Targets Python {{ python_version }}.

> **mypy is the authoritative type checker.** Editor LSPs (Pyright, Pylance, …)
> are welcome for inline hints, but only mypy's verdict gates the CI. If LSP
> and mypy disagree, mypy wins — adjust the code, don't relax the gate.

### Pylint — deeper linting

A second, more opinionated linter (`[tool.pylint]`) that catches design and
correctness issues Ruff does not. The hook fails the commit if the score drops
below 9; rules that overlap with Ruff are disabled to avoid duplicate noise.

### Bandit — security linting

Scans for common security issues (e.g. unsafe calls). Configured under
`[tool.bandit]`, with `.venv` and the built docs excluded so it does not scan
dependencies.

### Gitleaks &amp; hygiene hooks — secrets &amp; repo hygiene

Gitleaks scans for committed secrets; `detect-private-key` blocks key material;
the `pre-commit-hooks` set fixes whitespace/EOLs and validates YAML/TOML and merge
conflicts. Together they keep the repository clean and credential-free.

### interrogate — docstring coverage

Enforces a minimum docstring coverage on `src/` (`[tool.interrogate]`,
threshold 80 %). Hooked into pre-commit so undocumented public functions block
the commit. Tests and docs are excluded — they're not the contract.

### pytest — tests

The test runner (`[tool.pytest.ini_options]`), with `pytest-sugar` for nicer
output and `pytest-cov`/`coverage` for coverage (`[tool.coverage]`). Tests live in
`tests/`.
{% if include_sql %}
### sqlfluff — SQL linting (optional)

Lints and formats `.sql` files (`[tool.sqlfluff]`). A `dialect` is required and is
set to `{{ sql_dialect }}`; the hook only triggers when SQL files change.
{% endif %}
### MkDocs — documentation

Builds this site with the Material theme. `mkdocstrings` generates the API
reference from docstrings, and the changelog page is pulled straight from
`CHANGELOG.md`. Configured in `mkdocs.yml`.

### GitHub Actions &amp; Dependabot — CI and updates

`ci.yml` runs uv, the pre-commit checks, and pytest on every push and PR;
`docs.yml` deploys the docs to GitHub Pages. Dependabot proposes weekly updates
for dependencies and Actions.

### Agent files, skills &amp; env

`AGENTS.md` is the single source of truth for AI coding agents (read natively by
Claude Code, Codex CLI, Aider, and OpenCode). Tool-specific config files
(`.claude/`, `opencode.json`, `.aider.conf.yml`) only contain backend wiring —
behaviour rules live exclusively in `AGENTS.md` and `guidelines/`.

The Pareto skill set (`/explore`, `/spec`, `/plan`, `/test`, `/delegate`,
`/review`, `/verify`, `/debug`, `/capture`) lives in `skills/` in the open
[Agent Skills](https://agents.md) `SKILL.md` format. Each tool that supports
skill discovery loads them on demand.

Local secrets go in `.env` (git-ignored), copied from the tracked
`.env.template`. The template's `.env.template` is rendered with the right
hints for the backend(s) you selected (LiteLLM, Ollama, or subscription).
{% if include_sandbox %}
### The agent sandbox

An optional Docker-Compose sandbox runs the agents in a container with only the
repo mounted, so a hijacked agent can't reach host secrets. It is an additional
layer on top of the permission system, not a replacement — and it deliberately
does *not* block network egress. See [The agent sandbox](sandbox.md) for the
isolation model and [Run the sandbox](../how-to/run-the-sandbox.md) for commands.
{% endif %}
